<blockquote><p>
I send you a post-card which I received in the ordinary way by post from my brother, who lives at Sutton Scarsdale, a scattered village near Chesterfield. You will notice that the card was posted at 7.15 p.m. on the 5th October, and it was delivered during the evening of the following day. The address looks a mixture of Greek and German, but on inspection it will be found that each letter is spelled out in full. The pencilled words were inserted by the Post Office officials. The Post Office is often the object of complaints for tardiness in delivery, but I think great credit is due to it for its cleverness and promptness in this case. &#8212; Mr. John Alderson, 12, Albert Road, Stroud Green, N.
</p></blockquote>
<p>From the <a href="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v27%201904/page/116/mode/2up"><em>Strand</em></a>, January 1904. I make out the text as:</p>
<p>Hemr. Jay Hayhelldeehearesohhen<br />
12, Hayhellbehearetea Areohhaydee<br />
Steaareohyoudee Geearehehehen<br />
London N.</p>
